    {
      "id": "KA-S133-letter-nya",
      "sequence": 1231,
      "title": "ಞ (ña) — one character, and the stack it hides in",
      "headword": "ಞ",
      "romanization": "ña",
      "gloss": "the single character ಞ — a nasal you will nearly always meet stacked, never standing",
      "script": "kannada",
      "modality": "pen",
      "derivedModality": "pen",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "writing-type",
        "writing-block",
        "script-block"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:643f6fb739babe13",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "pen",
        "needs": [
          "a pen and something to write on",
          "your eyes, for letter shapes on the page"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [
          "Script you'll notice: ಞ",
          "Guided Practice"
        ],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your hands, so it is not a driving lesson. You will want a pen and something to write on and your eyes, for letter shapes on the page. You can listen to everything else now — leave the sections called Script you'll notice: ಞ and Guided Practice until you have stopped, and I will say so again when we reach it."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: ◌ಃ — what does it add, and which mark is its partner?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One character this time. You will hardly ever see it alone, and that fact is the lesson."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "Script you'll notice: ಞ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಞ — ña."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is a nasal, and you can already read two of its relatives:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ನ na — tongue at the teeth ridge, the sound in ನಮಸ್ಕಾರ namaskāra."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಣ ṇa — tongue curled back, the sound in ಗಂಟೆ gaṇṭe."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಞ ña — tongue flat against the roof of the mouth, the ny an English speaker hears in canyon."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kannada keeps these apart as separate letters where English writes n for all three and lets the neighbours decide."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Why you will not see it standing. Almost every time it appears it is stacked underneath another letter. The pair you will meet is:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಜ plus ಞ (ña) stacked gives ಜ್ಞ."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Learn that stack, because it is where this letter lives, and a word built on it is close at hand. Find the ಜ on top; what is squashed underneath is today's character. Expect it under another letter, not beside one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "writing",
          "title": "Writing: ಞ — copy what you see",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Put your pen on ಞ (ña) and follow its line. Copy the shape you can see — slowly, and larger than it is printed."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This book does not yet tell you where to start the character or which way to travel. That is a real thing, taught with real variation from school to school, and it is not written down here until it can be written down with a source. Copying what is in front of you needs no such source, and it is how the shape gets into your hand in the meantime."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "LOOK",
              "instruction": "at these three nasals and say which one is today's",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU LOOK: at these three nasals and say which one is today's]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ನ, ಣ, ಞ (ña)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "TRACE",
              "instruction": "ಞ three times, saying ña as in canyon as you finish each one",
              "spoken": false,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU TRACE: ಞ three times, saying *ña* as in canyon as you finish each one]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "LOOK",
              "instruction": "at ಜ್ಞ and say which letter sits on top and which is squashed beneath",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
